From 5dfd78e585b755a1fd968a1a19e7b52c65c7c0f7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Christopher Hotchkiss <chris@hotchkiss.io>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 12:33:21 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] O6: oracle chains for actors spawned mid-stretch
 (GUAC_ORACLE=1)

Two verbs on P1's AF_UNIX listener so O5's records follow actors that appear after a recording starts, at addresses no fixed range can name (openspec AH.5, design decision 16). Behind O3's gate: each answers P1's ERR unknown_cmd unless GUAC_ORACLE=1 is in the emulator's environment. Nothing knows the game: the client names every address, offset, value and name.

- CHAIN <slot> <items> <base> <path> <count> <array> <key> <values> [<range> ...] -> OK slot=<s> bytes=<b> listed=<n> kept=<k> | ERR bad_args | ERR bad_slot | ERR bad_items | ERR bad_hop | ERR bad_values | ERR bad_range | ERR too_big | ERR watching | ERR no_list. Arms chain <slot> (0-7), replacing the one there: from <base>, <path>'s hops (`-` for none) to a list whose count word sits at +<count> and its pointer to an array of item pointers at +<array>; each item whose <key> matches one of <values> (hex words joined by `,`, up to 64) is kept, up to <items> (1-512) a record, with its ranges (up to 32). A hop is `<off>` (the pointer there followed), `<off>=<word>` (and the object reached starts with that word) or `?<count>,<array>,<name>=<hash>` (a search through the list there for the element whose name, the C string the word at +<name> points at, hashes to <hash> by FNV-1a); a key is hops then `<off>` (the word there) or `#<off>` (the FNV-1a of the C string that word points at); a range is hops then `<off>+<len>` (1-1024 bytes). Offsets are 1-4 hex digits, words 1-8, hops 16 a probe. Refused while a watch is armed and when the path doesn't reach a list now (no_list). The block's 48 KiB budget is every chain's together.
- CHAIN_CLEAR -> OK | ERR bad_args | ERR watching. Every chain dropped.

Where it records: O5's record. WATCH takes the chains armed when it's armed, and every record it takes carries each one's block after the ranges, in slot order, so WATCH's bytes= and WATCH_READ's records grow by them. A block is a 20-byte header (the status: 0 when the list read, else the 1-based link that broke, the path's hops then the count, the array and a count past 4,096; the list's count; the items whose key didn't read; the items the filter matched; the items kept; each a big-endian u32), then each kept item in list order, 16 bytes (its address, its index in the list, the value it matched, a mask of its ranges that didn't read) and its ranges back to back, the rest of the block zeros. Every link is checked as it's followed (4-aligned, readable, not null, its word when one's named) and a range that doesn't read is zeros with its bit set. WATCH_CLEAR keeps the chains; a new emulation session drops them with the watch.

O5's ring budget grows from 16 MiB to 128 MiB and a WATCH_READ reply from 32 KiB of records to 256 KiB, so a record carrying its 16 KiB of ranges and 48 KiB of chains fits; O5's ranges stay capped at 16 KiB a record.

Touches cellPad.cpp (the chains beside O5's watch, followed at each record) and Skylander.cpp beside O3-O5.

